  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{y{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{y{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}[\\operatorname{tan}⁡x]_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡0)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|y|{|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
